// DISABLE_NATIVE: targetFamily=MINGW


// lib1 and lib2 both declare a top-level `val vf` in package p. The flat signature of a
// top-level klib declaration encodes only package+name (p/vf|{}vf[0]), so the two vals share
// one SymbolTable key: IdSignature carries no library identity.
//
// During the IC link, the spawned per-file cache build of lib2's b.kt first binds lib2's own
// vf in its frontend, then body lowering of callV() inlines lib1's public inline gv(), and
// the IC-specific InlineFunctionDeserializer (CacheSerializationSupport.kt:133) calls
// KonanIrLinker.deserializeAllReachableTopLevels(). Deserializing lib1's vf, which is
// reachable from gv()'s body, goes through IrDeclarationDeserializer.deserializeIrProperty
// -> SymbolTable.declareProperty -> SymbolTableSlice.declare (ir.tree SymbolTableSlice.kt:36),
// which finds the existing already-bound symbol (lib2's own val of the same signature) and
// still invokes the owner factory, so IrPropertyImpl.<init> -> IrSymbolBase.bind throws
// IllegalStateException "IrPropertySymbolImpl is already bound" -- an internal error
// ("Please report this problem"). Every IC link fails this way from a completely empty cache
// (dirty=0; there is no staleness), in the batched and the unbatched cache build alike, while
// a non-IC build of the same klibs links and runs: the non-IC link never deserializes lib2's
// unreferenced vf at all (it materializes only the vf pulled in through the inline body), so
// the two same-signature properties never collide there.
//
// The program itself does not need to reference the clashing API: the IC pipeline builds a
// per-file cache for every klib of the link, and the cache build of lib2 alone crashes. The
// getter form of both vals (no backing field) is load-bearing: with a backing field the
// module initializer references the property, the non-IC link deserializes both twins too,
// and the crash stops being IC-only.
//
// KT-89840's batch splitting (splitIntoNonConflictingGroups) logs "SPLITTING BATCH: lib2 and
// lib1 share a top-level signature: p/vf|..." but cannot help: the clash is between the
// frontend-bound own declaration and the deserialized dependency declaration inside lib2's
// own single-file compile, exactly the case that fix disclaims as pre-existing (KT-82172).

MODULES: lib1, lib2, main

STEP 0:
    libs: lib1, lib2, main
